WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.850
Network executives are paid to make the tough
decisions.

00:00:02.850 --> 00:00:07.090
And sometimes, they are also paid to make
weird and bizarre decisions, like canceling

00:00:07.090 --> 00:00:09.220
your favorite show for no apparent reason.

00:00:09.220 --> 00:00:12.650
If you ever thought showbusiness made sense
— well, think again.

00:00:12.650 --> 00:00:16.039
Here's a look at the dumb reasons these TV
shows were canceled.

00:00:16.039 --> 00:00:17.039
Police Squad!

00:00:17.039 --> 00:00:18.039
Goals

00:00:18.039 --> 00:00:22.040
Lots of movies are made out of TV shows, but
the three Naked Gun! movies probably mark

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:26.130
the first time a successful series of films
were spawned from an incredibly unpopular

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.370
TV show.

00:00:27.370 --> 00:00:28.730
"Nice beaver!"

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:31.930
"Thank you, I just had it stuffed."

00:00:31.930 --> 00:00:33.200
"Let me help you with that."

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:37.530
Filled with sight gags and recurring jokes,
Police Squad! starring Leslie Nielsen as Lieutenant

00:00:37.530 --> 00:00:42.890
Frank Drebin hit the airwaves in 1982...and
was pulled after just four episodes, with

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:45.139
the remaining two burned off over the summer.

00:00:45.139 --> 00:00:49.530
ABC president Tony Thomopoulos was surprisingly
frank about why people weren't tuning in.

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.469
He told a reporter that "the viewer had to
watch it to appreciate it."

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:57.089
That's right: Police Squad! asked that its
audience try that crazy thing where they pay

00:00:57.089 --> 00:00:59.640
attention to what's happening on the screen.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:03.889
Apparently the jokes on ABC's hit shows Happy
Days and The Love Boat were so simple they

00:01:03.889 --> 00:01:05.369
could be understood by osmosis.

00:01:05.369 --> 00:01:12.640
(gunshots)

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.190
Long in the tooth

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:18.930
Pulling in roughly three to five million viewers
per week, Longmire was A&amp;E's most-watched

00:01:18.930 --> 00:01:23.290
original drama ever, and was among the top
rated dramas on cable TV during its three

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:24.480
season run.

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.870
Yet the network canceled it anyway.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:27.870
Why?

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.750
Because most of those fans were too damn old!

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:34.850
Yes, TV networks are pretty much only interested
in courting the key 18 to 49 demographic,

00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:37.980
which advertisers think is the easiest group
to part from their money.

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:41.870
So because Longmire's audience wasn't gullible
enough, A&amp;E canceled it.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:45.170
No worries, though: the show was picked up
by Netflix, which doesn't have to worry about

00:01:45.170 --> 00:01:46.170
advertising trends.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.070
Too rural for primetime

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:54.630
During the 1969-1970 TV season, CBS chief
of programming Fred Silverman noticed that

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:59.570
the shows getting the best advertising rates
were ABC and NBC shows aimed at young and

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.010
hip viewers, with multicultural casts in urban
environments.

00:02:03.010 --> 00:02:07.700
CBS's shows, on the other hand, were mostly
rural family shows.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:12.040
Silverman's solution: cancel all his farm
shows and replace them with cutting edge new

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:13.609
city sitcoms.

00:02:13.609 --> 00:02:18.540
Out went The Red Skelton Show, Petticoat Junction,
Green Acres, Lassie, and the Jim Nabors Hour.

00:02:18.540 --> 00:02:19.540
Replacing them?

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.790
Honest to goodness classics, including The
Mary Tyler Moore Show, All in the Family,

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.720
Good Times, Maude, and The Bob Newhart Show.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:31.349
Ratings surged among younger demographics
and Silverman's move became Hollywood legend,

00:02:31.349 --> 00:02:34.229
known ever after as the great rural purge.

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.900
"Dynomite!"

00:02:35.909 --> 00:02:37.519
The wrong kind of smart

00:02:37.519 --> 00:02:42.599
For 26 years, LeVar Burton's beloved Reading
Rainbow helped kids develop a love of reading.

00:02:42.599 --> 00:02:47.109
But in 2009, budget restrictions forced the
show's cancelation because it wasn't educating

00:02:47.109 --> 00:02:48.999
kids in a government approved fashion.

00:02:48.999 --> 00:02:49.999
Weird, right?

00:02:49.999 --> 00:02:53.180
Well, it all started with the No Child Left
Behind Act.

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:58.540
Signed into law in 2002, it sought to establish
new educational benchmarks for American kids,

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.170
many of which would be measured with standardized
tests results.

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.990
"In that box is the bill.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:06.940
I don't intend to read it all."

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:13.760
That's fine for quantifiable skills, but it's
hard to measure "love of reading" on a standardized

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.860
test.

00:03:14.860 --> 00:03:16.239
So there went the funding!

00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:20.530
But hope is not lost: thanks to an incredibly
successful Kickstarter campaign, Reading Rainbow

00:03:20.530 --> 00:03:22.790
is poised for a 21st-century comeback.

00:03:22.790 --> 00:03:24.540
"I am overwhelmed.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.760
Thank you so much.

00:03:26.760 --> 00:03:31.569
This is going to enable us to really, really,
really do a lot of good."

00:03:31.569 --> 00:03:32.569
Thanks for watching!

00:03:32.569 --> 00:03:35.819
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:35.819 --> 00:03:39.120
And leave us a comment to let us know which
canceled show you think should be brought

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:39.620
back...

